      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-334032",
      ":question/problem": "A skydiver of mass $80kg$ free-falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the skydiver's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the skydiver is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k > 0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the skydiver are the weight due to gravity and drag."
