      ":question/problem": "A body of mass $12kg$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k > 0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body\u2019s velocity is $9m/s$ and instantaneous acceleration is $6.8m/s^{2}$.\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k$, and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nThe constant of proportionality is $k =$\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}.\n\nThe differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}."
